nodes.append({
 "id": 15, "lang": "Latin", "words": "carô; carni-levare",
 "verdict": "confirmed",
 "derivation": "Latin carō, carnis f. originally 'a piece, portion (of sacrificial meat)', then 'flesh' — the older sense is still visible in Oscan carneis 'partis', Umbrian karu 'pars', kartu 'distribuito' (Ernout-Meillet s.v. caro). It is an -n- enlargement of a root noun from *(s)ker- 'to cut', the root of Greek keírō 'I cut', Latin corium, curtus, cortex, with an s-initial doublet in OIr. scaraim 'I separate', OHG sceran 'to cut'. Medieval Latin carnelevāre / carnelevāmen 'the removing of meat', attested in Milanese carnelevale and Old Pisan carnelevare, named the last feast before the Lenten fast; Nourai's ☞Legwh points correctly to levāre 'to lift, take away' under a different root (PIE *legʷh- 'light in weight').",
